---
// Plain-language privacy policy. A solid draft, not legal advice:
// have it reviewed before relying on it. Fill the business details in src/lib/config.ts.
import LegalPage from '@/components/LegalPage.astro';
import PreviewNote from '@/components/PreviewNote.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, LEGAL_LOCATION, LEGAL_NAME } from '@/lib/config';

const operator = LEGAL_NAME || 'The Ludarium';
const missing = [!LEGAL_NAME && 'legal business name', !CONTACT_EMAIL && 'contact email', !LEGAL_LOCATION && 'state or country']
  .filter(Boolean)
  .join(', ');
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What The Ludarium collects when you join the newsletter, send an inquiry, or buy from the store, why we collect it, who helps us, and how to reach us."
  lede="What we collect, why, who helps us handle it, and how to reach us. No legalese where we can avoid it."
  updated="October 27, 2026"
>
  {
    missing && (
      <PreviewNote>
        Missing in src/lib/config.ts: {missing}. Also have a professional review this policy before the
        site launches on theludarium.com.
      </PreviewNote>
    )
  }

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    theludarium.com is run by {operator}{LEGAL_LOCATION && `, based in ${LEGAL_LOCATION}`}. In this policy,
    "we" and "us" mean The Ludarium.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <h3>When you join the newsletter or a waitlist</h3>
  <p>
    Your email address, and your first name if you give it. We also note which form you used (for
    example, the Indianapolis lounge waitlist) so we only send you what you asked for.
  </p>
  <h3>When you send us an inquiry</h3>
  <p>
    What you type into the form, such as your name, company, role, email, team size, goals, and timing
    for a corporate event.
  </p>
  <h3>When you buy from the store</h3>
  <p>
    Purchases are handled by Lemon Squeezy, which acts as the seller of record. Lemon Squeezy collects
    your payment details. We receive your name, email, and what you bought, but never your full card
    number.
  </p>
  <h3>When you visit the site</h3>
  <p>
    Our hosting provider, Cloudflare, processes basic technical information such as your IP address and
    browser type to deliver pages and protect the site from abuse. If you switch between light and dark
    mode, your browser remembers that choice on your device. It is never sent to us.
  </p>
  <h3>When you use the Card Catalogue</h3>
  <p>
    Your ratings, logged plays, shelves, and guests are stored only in your own browser on your
    device. We never receive them. A table code you choose to share contains your feel profile and game
    lists, never your notes or ratings, and goes only to the people you send it to.
  </p>
  <h3>When you use Where We Play</h3>
  <p>
    Where We Play helps a gaming group pick venues by drive time. If you share where you live, we
    store your email, your zip code, and, if you give them, your Discord handle, first name, street
    address, nearest cross street, and the days you can play. Only the app's admin can see your
    address, to work out your drive time to each candidate venue, and it is never shared or shown.
    Other members see only summaries, such as the median drive time to a venue for the group. We turn
    addresses into map points with OpenStreetMap's Nominatim service and get drive times from the
    OSRM routing project; both receive the address or coordinates and nothing about you. After you
    submit, you get a private link that lets you update or delete your entry at any time. We do not
    email it to you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We use <strong>Google Analytics</strong> to understand how people use the site: which articles
    and tools help, and how people find us. It records:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the pages you view and how long you stay</li>
    <li>how you arrived, such as a search, a link, or our newsletter</li>
    <li>your device type, browser, and approximate location (city level)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It does not record your name or email address, and Google Analytics does not store IP addresses.
    We do not use it for advertising: advertising features and Google Signals are turned off, and we
    keep analytics data for 14 months.
  </p>
  <h3>Where you are changes how it works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>In the United States and most countries,</strong> Google Analytics runs when you visit
      and sets cookies (named <code>_ga</code>) to recognize return visits. You can turn the cookies off
      anytime with <strong>Cookie settings</strong> at the bottom of every page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>In the European Union, the EEA, the United Kingdom, and Switzerland,</strong> we ask first.
      Until you click "Allow analytics," no analytics cookies are set. Google Analytics only receives a
      cookieless signal that a page was viewed, which cannot recognize you on a later visit.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you choose "No thanks," or your browser sends a <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong> signal, we
    treat it as an opt-out everywhere: no analytics cookies, only the cookieless signal described
    above. To decide where you are, the site asks Cloudflare for your country. That lookup is not
    stored.
  </p>
  <h3 id="game-counts">Our own game counts, with no cookies</h3>
  <p>
    We also keep our own count of how much interest each game gets on the site, so we can see which
    games are drawing attention and decide what to write and teach next. It sets no cookies and stores
    nothing on your device.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you spend about 15 seconds on a page about a game, or scroll halfway through it, your browser
    sends us a short note. The same happens when you download something about a game, sign up for a
    course, or click a store link for a game. The note holds only that event, the game, the page
    address, and, if the link that brought you names its source (such as our newsletter), that source.
  </p>
  <p>
    To count each person once a day without knowing who they are, our server mixes your IP address
    and browser type with a random value that changes every day, and keeps only a short scrambled code.
    Your IP address is never stored. Each night we add up the totals for each game, then delete the
    day's codes and that day's random value, so no code can be traced back to you or matched with
    another day. We keep only the daily totals per game, which say nothing about any person.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your browser sends a <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong> or <strong>Do Not Track</strong>
    signal, it sends us nothing at all. We also ignore known bots and visits from cloud data centers.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few other things are stored only in your own browser and never sent to us: your light or dark
    mode choice, your cookie choice, your country (so we do not have to look it up again), and your
    Card Catalogue cards. Sites you reach through
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate links</a> may set their own cookies under their own
    policies.
  </p>

  <h3>Your rights</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>We do not sell your personal information, and we do not share it for targeted
    advertising.</strong> Depending on where you live, you may have the right to know what we hold
    about you, to correct or delete it, and to opt out of analytics. Use Cookie settings for analytics,
    and contact us for anything else.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To send the emails you signed up for.</li>
    <li>To answer your questions and plan events you ask about.</li>
    <li>To deliver what you buy and help if something goes wrong.</li>
    <li>To keep the site running and secure.</li>
    <li>To learn which games readers care about most, from the cookieless daily counts above.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who helps us</h2>
  <p>We use a few trusted services to run The Ludarium:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kit</strong> sends our newsletter and stores subscriber details.</li>
    <li><strong>Lemon Squeezy</strong> handles store checkout, payments, and sales tax.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site, runs our cookieless game counts, stores Where We Play entries, and delivers inquiry notices to our inbox.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenStreetMap (Nominatim) and OSRM</strong> turn Where We Play addresses into map points and drive times.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Analytics</strong> measures how the site is used, as described above.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    They only use your information to provide their service to us. <strong>We never sell your personal
    information</strong>, and we never share it for advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every email has an unsubscribe link. One click and you are out.</li>
    <li>You can ask us to show you, correct, or delete the information we hold about you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    We keep subscriber details while you are subscribed, inquiry details for as long as we are talking
    with you about an event, and purchase records as long as tax law requires. The scrambled codes
    behind our game counts last one day at most; the daily totals per game, which hold no personal
    information, we keep. Where We Play entries stay until you delete them with your private link or
    ask us to.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The Ludarium is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect their
    information. If you think a child has signed up, contact us and we will remove it.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>If we change this policy, we will update the date at the top of this page.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    {
      CONTACT_EMAIL ? (
        <>
          Questions or requests: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </>
      ) : (
        <>Questions or requests: reply to any email from The Ludarium.</>
      )
    }
  </p>
</LegalPage>
